record.design_notes:
1) TOPOLOGY (one host, one origin)
Browser (Chrome) → https://<domain> → [web: Caddy 2.11.4] routes three ways:
- /assets/*, /models/*, index.html, sw.js, version.json → static shell baked into the web image;
- /api/* → app:3000 (HTTPS: auth, bulk transfer, LLM proxy);
- /ws → app:3000 (WebSocket: sync, locks, process/change events).
The app talks to db:5432 (Postgres 18.6 + pgvector) and blobs:3900 (Garage S3) over the private network only. Off-site copies go to Cloudflare R2 (dumps and blobs). Serving everything from one origin makes crossOriginIsolated=true trivial (MEM-01), keeps the Service Worker scope simple (OFF-05), and avoids CORS for the WebSocket.

2) COMPOSE (compose.yaml, shared by examiner, CI and prod)
```yaml
name: plotinium
services:
  db:
    image: pgvector/pgvector:0.8.6-pg18-trixie
    environment: {POSTGRES_USER: plotinium, POSTGRES_DB: plotinium, POSTGRES_PASSWORD: ${DB_PASSWORD:-dev-only-not-secret}}
    volumes: [pgdata:/var/lib/postgresql]   # PG18 images changed the data-dir layout; confirm the path in the image README
    healthcheck: {test: [CMD-SHELL, pg_isready -U plotinium], interval: 2s, retries: 30}
  blobs:
    image: dxflrs/garage:v2.4.1
    command: [/garage, server, --single-node, --default-bucket]
    environment: {GARAGE_DEFAULT_ACCESS_KEY: ${S3_KEY:-GKdevonly0000000000000000000000}, GARAGE_DEFAULT_SECRET_KEY: ${S3_SECRET:-devonly...}, GARAGE_DEFAULT_BUCKET: plotinium, GARAGE_RPC_SECRET: ${GARAGE_RPC_SECRET:-<64 hex dev value>}}
    volumes: [./ops/garage.toml:/etc/garage.toml:ro, garage-meta:/var/lib/garage/meta, garage-data:/var/lib/garage/data]
  init:            # one-shot: migrate up + demo seed (idempotent)
    image: ghcr.io/<owner>/plotinium-server:${PLOTINIUM_VERSION:-1.0.0}
    build: {context: ., target: server}
    command: [node, server/dist/init.js, --migrate, --seed=demo]
    depends_on: {db: {condition: service_healthy}, blobs: {condition: service_started}}
  app:
    image: ghcr.io/<owner>/plotinium-server:${PLOTINIUM_VERSION:-1.0.0}
    build: {context: ., target: server}
    env_file: {path: .env, required: false}   # LLM keys only in prod .env; absent → AI degrades gracefully
    depends_on: {init: {condition: service_completed_successfully}}
    healthcheck: {test: [CMD, node, server/dist/health.js], interval: 2s, retries: 30}
  web:
    image: ghcr.io/<owner>/plotinium-web:${PLOTINIUM_VERSION:-1.0.0}   # caddy:2.11.4-alpine + dist/
    build: {context: ., target: web}
    ports: ["8080:8080"]
    depends_on: {app: {condition: service_healthy}}
volumes: {pgdata: {}, garage-meta: {}, garage-data: {}}
```
When a service has both `image` and `build`, Compose tries the pull first and builds from source only if the pull fails, so examiners on arm64 Macs without a matching image still get a working stack. Verify during the spike that `up --wait` accepts the exited-0 init container on Compose v5.5; otherwise wait on `web` explicitly.

compose.prod.yaml overrides:
- web gets ports 80, 443 and 443/udp plus `DOMAIN`;
- every secret uses `${VAR:?required}`;
- `restart: unless-stopped`;
- `logging: {driver: local, options: {max-size: 10m, max-file: "5"}}`;
- mem limits (db 2.5 GB, app 1.5 GB, blobs 512 MB);
- Postgres `-c shared_buffers=1GB -c effective_cache_size=4GB -c maintenance_work_mem=512MB` (final values belong to the DB owner).

README bring-up:
(1) `git clone https://github.com/<owner>/plotinium.git && cd plotinium`
(2) `docker compose up -d --wait`
(3) open http://localhost:8080 (demo credentials are printed by init)
Reset with `docker compose down -v`. Use localhost rather than a LAN IP: http://localhost counts as a secure context (Service Worker, OPFS, crypto.subtle), a LAN IP over plain http does not.

3) CADDYFILE (prod; the local file is the same with `:8080` in place of the domain)
```
{$DOMAIN} {
  encode zstd gzip
  header {
    Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy same-origin
    Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy require-corp
    Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy same-origin
    Content-Security-Policy "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval'; worker-src 'self' blob:; connect-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data: blob:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'"
    Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
    X-Content-Type-Options nosniff
    Referrer-Policy no-referrer
    -Server
  }
  @immutable path /assets/* /models/*
  header @immutable Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
  @fresh path /sw.js /index.html /version.json /manifest.webmanifest
  header @fresh Cache-Control "no-cache"
  handle /ws  { reverse_proxy app:3000 { lb_try_duration 15s  stream_close_delay 5m } }
  handle /api/* { reverse_proxy app:3000 { lb_try_duration 15s  lb_try_interval 250ms  health_uri /readyz } }
  handle { root * /srv  try_files {path} /index.html  file_server { precompressed br zstd gzip } }
}
```
Because the CSP has no 'unsafe-eval', eval and new Function are blocked by the platform itself, backing up AGT-01 ('never eval'). 'wasm-unsafe-eval' is there only for the embedding runtime.

4) RELEASE, DEPLOY AND OFF-05
- Build args VERSION, GIT_SHA and BUILD_DATE are baked into version.json and a kernel constant, so `uname -a` prints e.g. `Plotinium 1.0.0 (a1b2c3d) 2026-12-20` (§5 identity).
- release.yml runs on tag v*: full CI, e2e and AI eval with recorded responses → multi-arch images to GHCR (X.Y.Z and sha tags) → a clean-bring-up job that fails at >240 s → GitHub Release with generated notes, coverage (json-summary + HTML) and perf report (§17.2) → the deploy job, which waits for approval in `environment: production` from a member other than the tagger, then runs ops/deploy.sh vX.Y.Z over SSH with a pinned known_hosts.
- deploy.sh steps: git checkout tag → backup.sh --tag pre-vX.Y.Z → compose pull → `compose run --rm init --migrate` (expand-only migrations) → `compose up -d --wait --no-deps app web` → smoke check (/readyz, and version.json must equal the tag) → image prune.
- Rollback is `deploy.sh v<previous>`: images are immutable, and the contract step of a migration ships only in the release after that.
- Client/server skew: the WebSocket hello carries {protocol, build}. The server accepts protocol N and N-1; for N-2 it replies UPGRADE_REQUIRED.
- Service Worker: at boot (POST stage) it calls registration.update(). A new SW precaches into shell-<version> in the background and stays `waiting` (no skipWaiting mid-session). The taskbar shows 'Restart to update', and the new SW activates at the next boot (OFF-05). Old caches are deleted on activate.
- Locks survive deploys: they live in the `locks` table with lease_expires_at (for example a 30 s TTL with 10 s heartbeats), and clients re-heartbeat after reconnecting with their last seq (VFS-09, OFF-02).
- Demo script for OFF-05: v1.0.0 is live; deploy v1.0.1 (a bug fix, so it is freeze-compliant); the open desktop shows the update badge; `reboot`; uname shows 1.0.1.

5) OBJECT STORAGE DESIGN
```ts
interface BlobStore {
  put(key: string, body: Readable | Uint8Array, o: { size: number; sha256: string }): Promise<void>; // hashes while streaming; mismatch → EIO, object deleted
  get(key: string, range?: { start: number; end: number }): Promise<Readable>;
  head(key: string): Promise<{ size: number } | null>;
  delete(keys: string[]): Promise<void>;
  presignGet?(key: string, ttlSec: number): Promise<string>; // later optimisation
}
```
- Implementations: S3BlobStore (Garage, R2), FsBlobStore (tests and fallback) and FaultyBlobStore (throws after N operations, used by the fault-injection harness).
- Keys: `blobs/sha256/ab/cd/<sha256>` is content-addressed (dedupe through blobs.sha256 UNIQUE and refcount). `swap/<uid>/<session>/<pid>/<page_id>` holds swap pages, with Garage lifecycle Expiration after 7 days for abandoned sessions; swap is never backed up.
- Ordering rule (no two-phase commit): upload the blob first (idempotent), then run ONE DB transaction that inserts or increments the blobs row and writes the inode. A rollback leaves only an unreferenced object. Refcount decrements happen in the transaction; the GC sweeps an object only when refcount = 0 AND zero_since is older than 14 days, which is more than 2× the backup interval, so any dump from the last week still finds its blobs.
- v1 streams bytes through the app so the permission and quota checks live in one place (a 5 MB stream does not block the event loop). The pager worker compresses pages with CompressionStream('gzip'), and the checksum is the sha256 of the uncompressed bytes, so MEM-03 byte-identity is preserved.
- Switch to presigned GETs on an `s3.` subdomain with CORS only if app CPU passes 50% during transfers or remote page-in p95 passes 1.5 s.

6) CI PIPELINES (GitHub Actions)
ci.yml runs on pull_request and push to main, with `concurrency: {group: ci-${{ github.ref }}, cancel-in-progress: true}`.
- check (~2 min): `pnpm i --frozen-lockfile` with the store cached, `biome ci .`, `tsc -b` (TS 7), gitleaks.
- unit (~3 min): `vitest run --project unit --coverage`, where vitest.config sets `coverage.thresholds = {lines: 70, 'dsa/**': {lines: 85}, 'vfs/**': {lines: 85}, 'kernel/**': {lines: 85}}` and reporters include json-summary; a PR comment via davelosert/vitest-coverage-report-action@v2.13.0; fast-check property tests with the seed printed on failure.
- integration (~6 min): `docker compose up -d --wait db blobs`, then:
  - migrations up→down→up round-trip on every PR touching db/ (DB-01);
  - syscalls against the DB;
  - the fault-injection matrix (rm -r, mv, cp -r, batch rename, chmod -R on a 1,000-node tree failing at node 500: tree checksum and quota unchanged, orphan probes = 0) (DB-02);
  - concurrent overlapping moves (DB-03), lock lease expiry (VFS-09), outbox exactly-once replay (OFF-02).
  Compose is used here rather than service containers because service containers cannot pass Garage's command arguments, and this way CI also tests the examiner path.
- e2e (main and tags, ~8 min): the full stack built from the commit, Playwright with channel 'chrome': boot → login → terminal → file ops → reboot → session restored; context.setOffline(true) + reload → usable desktop (BOOT-09/OFF-01); assert crossOriginIsolated.
- Required status checks on main: check, unit, integration. Also one approving review from another member, CODEOWNERS mapping each subsystem to its primary owner plus secondary reviewer, and no force pushes.

nightly.yml runs on cron '30 21 * * *' (02:30 PKT) and skips when main has not changed in 24 h. Perf suite, 5 repetitions each, median of the p95s:
- warm/cold boot (BOOT-10);
- frame time while dragging 10 windows during a CPU-bound process (WM-03/PROC-05);
- trie p95 at ≥10k entries (SYS-03);
- Q1–Q5 p95 at 100k inodes with EXPLAIN ANALYZE captured (DB-05);
- 5 MB local page-in (MEM-05);
- retrieval p95 over 10k chunks (RAG-05).
CDP Emulation.setCPUThrottlingRate is calibrated to the lab machine. perf/check-budgets.ts compares results against perf/budgets.json and exits non-zero on any breach. Results are uploaded as artifacts and written to the job summary.

Minutes projection for a private repo: about 11 per PR run × 150 + about 8 × 60 main merges + about 20 × 22 nightlies ≈ 2,570/month. That fits Pro or Team (3,000) but not Free (2,000). A public repo has no limit.

Task runner: none. Use `pnpm -r --filter "...[origin/main]" test`. Adopt Turborepo 2.11 only on the CI wall-time trigger. Nx is rejected.

7) OBSERVABILITY (lean)
- pino fields: ts, level, msg, build, reqId, sessionId, uid, pid, syscall, errno, durMs. Redacted: authorization, cookie, password, token, and args.content.
- /metrics is internal only; Caddy never routes it:
  - ws_connections;
  - ws_messages_total{dir,type};
  - lock_grant_seconds (histogram; the §14.3-style evidence);
  - lock_wait_total;
  - op_apply_seconds{op};
  - op_replay_batch_size;
  - db_tx_seconds{op};
  - db_tx_retries_total{reason};
  - blob_io_seconds{op};
  - swap_bytes_total{dir};
  - llm_tokens_total{model,kind} and llm_cost_usd_total{model} (feeding the s12.6 cost report and budget cap);
  - disk_free_bytes;
  - Node defaults (RSS, heap, event-loop lag).
- /healthz reports liveness. /readyz runs `SELECT 1`, a HeadBucket against Garage, and a migration-version check.
- Client side, the kernel's main-thread shim observes PerformanceObserver({type: 'long-animation-frame', buffered: true}). Entries whose scripts[].sourceURL is our origin go into the kernel log ring buffer (extra-credit ring buffer) and are visible in dmesg, bootlog and Task Manager. Together with a frame-meter overlay this provides the 'no long task >50 ms from OS code' evidence for PROC-05.
- window.onerror, unhandledrejection and worker errors go to the kernel log. Only with an explicit Settings → Diagnostics opt-in (in line with PRE-05) are they uploaded in batches to POST /api/diag, where pino records them with the build hash.
- Source maps are kept as private CI artifacts and never served.
- The upgrade path is a Compose `ops` profile (Prometheus v3.13 + Grafana 13, with GlitchTip 6.2 reusing our Postgres) that needs no app changes.

8) BACKUPS AND RESTORE DRILL (DB-01)
- ops/backup.sh runs from a host systemd timer at 02:00 PKT and before every deploy: `docker compose exec -T db pg_dump -Fc -Z zstd:6 -U plotinium plotinium > /var/backups/plotinium/db-<date>.dump`, plus a sidecar JSON holding the fsck tree checksum and row counts. It then runs `rclone copy` of the dumps to R2 and `rclone sync` of the Garage bucket to R2 (incremental, since blobs are content-addressed). Retention: 7 daily + 4 weekly.
- ops/restore-drill.sh runs weekly and before every gate. It starts a throwaway pgvector:0.8.6-pg18 container, runs `pg_restore -j4`, then the db/probes/*.sql checks (orphaned inodes = 0, parentless dentries = 0, FK validation, recomputed quotas equal stored quotas), compares the tree checksum with the sidecar, and prints the RTO. It sets backup_restore_drill_last_success_timestamp. Results go into the DATABASE.md table.

9) SECURITY AND HOST HARDENING
- Ubuntu 24.04, unattended-upgrades, key-only SSH, and a `deploy` user in the docker group.
- The Hetzner Cloud Firewall (free, outside the VM) allows 22/tcp, 80/tcp and 443/tcp+udp. Do not rely on UFW, because Docker-published ports bypass it.
- /srv/plotinium/.env is mode 0600 and lives on the VPS only; LLM keys exist nowhere else.
- GitHub 'production' environment secrets: SSH_KEY and KNOWN_HOSTS.
- The server refuses to start in production if any secret equals its dev default or is shorter than 32 bytes.
- The domain comes from the Student Pack (Namecheap .me or .TECH, free for one year), with DNS A and AAAA records pointing at the VPS.

10) REPO GOVERNANCE
- Ask the instructor in week 1. Private repos need GitHub Pro (Student Pack, personal owner) or a teacher's free GitHub Team org for branch protection and 3,000 minutes. A public repo would get free 4-vCPU runners and unlimited minutes, but makes copying by other teams easier.
- Tags per gate: v0.2.0 (W7), v0.3.0 (W11), v0.4.0 (W14), v1.0.0-rc.1 (W16), v1.0.0 (W18). CHANGELOG.md is updated in each release PR, commits follow conventional feat:/fix:/test:/docs: messages referencing requirement IDs, and GitHub-generated notes are attached to each Release.

11) BUDGET (Oct–Feb)
- Hetzner CX33 at about €9/month ×5 ≈ €45. The spike uses hourly CX23s, under €1.
- Domain €0 (Student Pack), R2 €0 (≤10 GB), GitHub €0.
- Contingency: Actions overage at $0.006/min.
- Total about €45–50, roughly €10 per student.
RAM plan on 8 GB: Postgres ≈2–2.5 GB, app ≤1.5 GB, Garage ≈0.2 GB, Caddy ≈0.05 GB, OS/Docker ≈0.5 GB, leaving more than 3 GB of headroom for HNSW builds and dumps. Measure this in the spike.

12) CROSS-DECISION DEPENDENCIES
- The backend-runtime decision sets the app base image (Node 24 LTS or Bun 1.4).
- The UI-framework decision sets whether any package pins TS 6.0.3 (Svelte and Vue checkers need it; React and Solid work with TS 7).
- The DB/ORM decision's migration tool must support down migrations; otherwise use dbmate 2.36.
- The AI decision must keep model weights same-origin and lazy-loaded after the desktop is interactive, and must degrade gracefully when .env has no LLM key, which is the examiner bring-up case.
